<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Muldoon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1921">1921</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Robert Muldoon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Muldoon was Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> from 1975 to 1984 and also his own finance minister. Leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>, he responded to oil shocks and loss of British markets with heavy state intervention, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/think-big">Think Big</a> energy projects and a wage and price freeze from 1982. His combative style divided the country, and he lost the snap election he called in 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-muldoon.jpg" alt="Robert Muldoon" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archives New Zealand from New Zealand · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Muldoon_1978.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert David Muldoon was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> on 25 September 1921. When he was eight his father was admitted to Auckland Mental Hospital, and he was raised by his mother under the strong influence of his maternal grandmother Jerusha, a committed socialist whose creed he never accepted but who left him with an interest in politics and a respect for the welfare state. He left Mount Albert Grammar School at 15 and studied accountancy by correspondence. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he served in the South Pacific and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where he sat his final accountancy examinations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After two unsuccessful bids, he won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> seat of Tamaki for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> in 1960 and held it for 32 years. As under-secretary to the finance minister Harry Lake he oversaw the introduction of decimal currency in July 1967. On Lake's death in February 1967 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keith-holyoake">Keith Holyoake</a> made him Minister of Finance, at 45 the youngest since the 1890s; he met a slump in wool prices with mini-budgets, spending cuts and higher indirect taxes. He became deputy prime minister in 1972 and, after National's defeat that year, was elected unopposed as Leader of the Opposition on 9 July 1974, drawing loyal crowds known as Rob's Mob.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Sworn in on 12 December 1975, he named himself Minister of Finance and promised a government of the ordinary bloke. A recession from June 1976 to March 1978 shrank the economy by 4.1%, and his years in power saw stagnation, high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a>, rising unemployment and heavy borrowing. His government introduced national superannuation and industrial incentives, revived and intensified the Dawn Raids against Pasifika overstayers, and kept close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anzus">ANZUS</a>. His refusal to stop the Springbok rugby tour of 1981 brought civil disorder on a scale the country had not seen.</p><p>National won again in 1978 and 1981 while losing the popular vote. In 1982 Derek Quigley resigned from the Cabinet after criticising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/think-big">Think Big</a>, and the businessman Bob Jones founded the New Zealand Party in 1983. Muldoon lost the snap election of 1984 and was then replaced as leader by his deputy Jim McLay. Heavy legal costs led him to appear in The Rocky Horror Show. He stayed in parliament until 1992 and died on 5 August that year; Black Power performed a haka at his funeral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Muldoon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Robert Muldoon</b> (1975–1984) combined heavy state intervention, including the “Think Big” energy projects and a wage and price freeze, with a combative style.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1976 Garth Henry Latta of Dunedin petitioned Parliament, and on 21 November 1977, with the consent of Queen Elizabeth II, <b>Robert Muldoon</b>'s government gazetted it as New Zealand's second national anthem, equal in status with &quot;God Save the Queen&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-36.85,174.77&amp;year=1967" aria-label="Auckland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Robert Muldoon"><image href="/maps/globes/g180s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4642 5593h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4642 5593h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-36.85,174.77&amp;year=1967">What was here in 1967? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
